    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1090">

                <pb facs="0961" n="857" sameAs="frus:frus1913/pg857"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/9625.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Ambassador to
                            Italy to the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1913-11-08T20:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 8, 1913—8 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Department’s November 7, 10 p.m. Minister for Foreign Affairs states that he has
                    the greatest confidence in the good intention of the President, and knows that
                    the United States would protect Italian interests, but he does not see what
                    practical action Italy can take. It would be a violation of neutrality to make
                    public expression of sympathy with the United States, and might jeopardize the
                    interests of Italy which are purely commercial. He added that Huerta was no
                    worse than the others and the only person in sight apparently strong enough to
                    restore order of some kind.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nelson Page</hi>.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
